/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (      C\) SERVO MOTOR  Servo Motor is a rotator device that allows the control of linear as well as angular motion. A Servo Motor is used for the opening and closing of the gate. Servo drive sends electrical signals to the Servo Motor for producing motion.         Types of Servo Motors There are various types of servo motors, differing in size, weight, and torque \(force\). The photograph below shows a very typical servo motor in modeling: the Futaba S3003. A little further down the article, we will use another servo motor commonly referred to as a "9-gram servo motor" due to its electrical consumption.)
/E (      C\) SERVO MOTOR  Servo Motor is a rotator device that allows the control of linear as well as angular motion. A Servo Motor is used for the opening and closing of the gate. Servo drive sends electrical signals to the Servo Motor for producing motion.         Types of Servo Motors There are various types of servo motors, differing in size, weight, and torque \(force\). The photograph below shows a very typical servo motor in modeling: the Futaba S3003. A little further down the article, we will use another servo motor commonly referred to as a "9-gram servo motor" due to its electrical consumption.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
524 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (       Operating Principle of a Servo Motor Most servo motors are controlled through a three-wire electrical cable that powers the motor and transmits position commands in the form of a pulse width modulation \(PWM\) signal. This means that the duration of the pulses determines the absolute angle of the output shaft and thus the position of the servo motor's control arm. The signal is repeated periodically, typically every 20 milliseconds, allowing the electronics to continuously control and correct the angular position of the output shaft, which is measured by the potentiometer.)
/E (       Operating Principle of a Servo Motor Most servo motors are controlled through a three-wire electrical cable that powers the motor and transmits position commands in the form of a pulse width modulation \(PWM\) signal. This means that the duration of the pulses determines the absolute angle of the output shaft and thus the position of the servo motor's control arm. The signal is repeated periodically, typically every 20 milliseconds, allowing the electronics to continuously control and correct the angular position of the output shaft, which is measured by the potentiometer.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
